$\begin{align}
2^3\cdot 2^7&=2^{3+7}
\\\\
&=2^{10}
\end{align}$

This follows from the general rule $x^m\cdot x^n=x^{m+n}$. Note that the powers have the same base.

We can also see this is correct by expanding the powers.

$\begin{align}
2^3\cdot 2^7&=\underbrace{2\cdot 2\cdot 2}_\text{3 times}\cdot\underbrace{2\cdot 2\cdot 2\cdot 2\cdot 2\cdot 2\cdot 2}_\text{7 times}
\\\\\\
&=\underbrace{2\cdot 2\cdot 2\cdot 2\cdot 2\cdot 2\cdot 2\cdot 2\cdot 2\cdot 2}_\text{10 times}
\\\\
&=2^{10}
\end{align}$
